UPDATE stage_prompts SET config = '{"stage":4,"laneTitle":"Scene Blueprint","note":"Combines the old Stage 4 (Scene) and Stage 5 (Director Shot List) into one stage, per the reference doc — one item per scene carrying both dramatic content and camera/shot fields, one deliverable image, one payment gate instead of two. Item key stays ''scene''; the old standalone ''shotlist'' item type is retired when the code catches up to this json.","assemblyOrder":["masterSheetPrompt","yourDescription","universalStyle"],"universalStyle":{"key":"production_bible_universal","label":"Universal Production Bible Style","text":"GLOBAL ART DIRECTION\nApple keynote quality, A24 minimalism, Netflix production documentation, Weta Workshop presentation, Cannes Lions pitch deck.\nPremium overlays, typography, spacing, version labels, production numbering.\nEvery image should feel like a real frame from a multi-million-dollar commercial."},"items":{"scene":{"label":"Scene","note":"fieldsSchema keeps only what actually affects the Stage 4 keyframe image — Duration/Transition/B-roll/Movement moved to Stage 5 (video-editing/motion concepts a still frame can''t represent), Objective removed (was never used by the compiled prompt at all). content.geminiInstruction removed — Scene content (location/action/dialogue/emotion) now comes directly from Stage 1 via the Story sync, and shot/camera fields are designer-set directly. Nothing regenerates this content via Gemini anymore.","content":{"fieldsSchema":[{"key":"location","label":"Location","type":"text"},{"key":"action","label":"Action","type":"text"},{"key":"dialogue","label":"Dialogue","type":"textarea"},{"key":"emotion","label":"Emotion","type":"pill","options":["Frustrated","Curious","Relieved","Happy","Excited","Confident","Calm"],"optionHelp":{"Frustrated":"What: a mix of annoyance and being stuck. When to use: for a \"before\" moment showing a problem or pain point. Example: struggling to open a stubborn jar lid.","Curious":"What: an inquisitive, exploring expression. When to use: when introducing a new product or discovery. Example: examining a new gadget for the first time.","Relieved":"What: tension easing into comfort. When to use: right after a problem gets solved. Example: exhaling after finally getting a stain out.","Happy":"What: a warm, pleased, genuine smile. When to use: for a satisfied \"after\" moment. Example: smiling after the first sip of coffee.","Excited":"What: energetic, enthusiastic delight. When to use: for a big reveal or standout result. Example: reacting to a surprise unboxing.","Confident":"What: assured, composed, in-control. When to use: for an expert or authority moment. Example: a chef presenting a finished dish with certainty.","Calm":"What: relaxed, at ease, unhurried. When to use: for a soothing or wind-down moment. Example: sinking into a chair with a cup of tea."}},{"key":"type","label":"Shot type","type":"pill","options":["Close-up","Wide","Macro","Medium","POV","Over-the-shoulder"],"optionHelp":{"Close-up":"What: tight framing on a face or small detail. When to use: to show emotion or fine detail. Example: a close-up on someone''s face reacting to a taste.","Wide":"What: a broad shot showing the full scene or environment. When to use: to establish location and context. Example: showing a whole kitchen before zooming into the action.","Macro":"What: an extreme close-up on tiny detail or texture. When to use: to highlight fine product detail. Example: droplets forming on a chilled can.","Medium":"What: frames a subject from roughly waist-up. When to use: the standard \"talking\" shot, balances subject and surroundings. Example: someone speaking directly to camera at a counter.","POV":"What: point-of-view — the camera sees through the subject''s own eyes. When to use: to put the viewer directly in the moment. Example: looking down at your own hands opening a package.","Over-the-shoulder":"What: framed from behind one subject, looking past them at another person or object. When to use: for conversations or handing something over. Example: looking over a customer''s shoulder as they unbox a product."}},{"key":"camera_angle","label":"Camera Angle","type":"pill","options":["Eye Level","Low Angle","High Angle","Top View","Dutch Angle"],"optionHelp":{"Eye Level":"What: camera at the subject''s natural eye height. When to use: the neutral, most natural-feeling default. Example: a straightforward product shot on a table.","Low Angle":"What: camera positioned below, looking up. When to use: to make a subject feel powerful or larger-than-life. Example: looking up at a bottle to make it feel premium.","High Angle":"What: camera positioned above, looking down. When to use: to make a subject feel small or vulnerable, or to show an overview. Example: looking down at ingredients laid out on a counter.","Top View":"What: directly overhead, looking straight down. When to use: for flat-lay or overview shots. Example: an overhead shot of a full table spread.","Dutch Angle":"What: camera tilted off the horizontal axis. When to use: for tension, unease, or stylized energy. Example: a tilted shot during a dramatic or chaotic moment."}},{"key":"camera","label":"Camera","type":"pill","options":["Handheld","Tripod","Gimbal","Drone"],"optionHelp":{"Handheld":"What: naturally shaky, human-held camera movement. When to use: for a raw, authentic, documentary feel. Example: a casual lifestyle moment filmed like a friend recording it.","Tripod":"What: fully static, no camera movement. When to use: for a clean, stable, formal shot. Example: a straightforward product-on-table shot.","Gimbal":"What: smooth, stabilized motion while moving. When to use: for polished walking or following shots with no shake. Example: smoothly following someone walking into a room.","Drone":"What: aerial camera movement from above. When to use: for sweeping establishing shots or a sense of scale. Example: an aerial view descending onto a rooftop cafe."}},{"key":"lens","label":"Lens","type":"pill","options":["24mm","35mm","50mm","85mm"],"optionHelp":{"24mm":"What: a wide-angle lens, captures a broad field of view. When to use: for establishing shots or tight spaces. Example: showing a whole room in one shot.","35mm":"What: slightly wide, natural-feeling everyday lens. When to use: general-purpose scenes with some surrounding context. Example: a person in their kitchen with surroundings visible.","50mm":"What: standard lens, closest to natural human-eye perspective. When to use: for balanced, natural-looking portraits or product shots. Example: a straightforward, natural-looking product close-up.","85mm":"What: telephoto, compresses the background and isolates the subject. When to use: for flattering close-up portraits with a blurred background. Example: a face close-up with a soft, blurred background."}}]},"outputInstructions":[{"key":"master_scene_blueprint_prompt","label":"Master Scene Blueprint Prompt","default":true,"text":"Create a premium Scene Blueprint based ONLY on the scene description provided below.\n\nOBJECTIVE\nVisualize the described scene exactly as written.\nGenerate a photorealistic cinematic frame suitable for client approval before video production.\nDo not invent story elements.\n\nREFERENCE LOCK (critical)\nAny Character, Property, or Background referenced below (by name, description, and attached reference image/file name) is APPROVED and LOCKED — this scene must depict them with the exact same face, body, outfit, colors, materials, and location/lighting/style already established for them. Do not redesign, restyle, or reinterpret any of them, even if this scene''s camera angle differs from their reference sheet. Reference each attached file by its exact file name so Flow knows which attachment applies to what.\n\nSTYLE\n• Hollywood commercial frame\n• Premium advertising photography\n• Cinematic realism\n• Feature-film lighting\n• Luxury editorial presentation\n• Cannes Lions quality\n\nCOMPOSITION\n• Rule of thirds\n• Leading lines\n• Foreground/Midground/Background separation\n• Natural depth\n• Cinematic color grading\n• Motivated lighting\n\nDO NOT\nChange story. Change any referenced character/prop/background''s established appearance. No cartoon, anime or comic styles. No text, captions, subtitles, labels, watermarks, or graphic overlays of any kind — this must be a clean photographic frame with nothing printed on it, since this same image is later used as the reference frame for animating the Final Movie clip.\n\nDIMENSIONS\nRender at high resolution (minimum 1600px on the longest side), in the aspect ratio matching the target platform — vertical 9:16 for Reels/Shorts/TikTok, 16:9 for YouTube/landscape.\n\nOUTPUT\nOne premium Scene Blueprint image for commercial pre-production, faithful to every referenced character/prop/background''s locked appearance — a clean photographic frame with no text or graphic overlays."}]}}}' WHERE stage = 4;
UPDATE stage_prompts SET config = '{"stage":5,"laneTitle":"Final Movie","note":"Kept per-scene, as confirmed — each scene generates its own clip independently; stitching all scene clips into one finished commercial happens externally, outside this tool. The source doc describes a single whole-project ''Final Production Render'' assembling every approved stage at once — adapted below to per-scene scope: ''this scene''s Blueprint/Character/Prop/Background Sheets + Music Brief'' instead of the whole project''s. Cross-scene concerns (adding new scenes, transitions between scenes) trimmed out since they don''t apply to a single clip.","assemblyOrder":["masterSheetPrompt","yourDescription","universalStyle"],"universalStyle":{"key":"production_bible_universal","label":"Universal Production Bible Style","text":"Maintain one consistent visual identity.\n\n• Apple keynote presentation quality\n• Cannes Lions commercial quality\n• Netflix production consistency\n• Weta Workshop attention to detail\n\nThe final video must feel like it was directed by an award-winning commercial director and produced by a premium production house."},"items":{"movie":{"label":"Final Movie Clip","note":"content.geminiInstruction removed — animation direction is now a plain designer-set field, no Gemini drafting. Transition/B-roll moved here from Stage 4 — they''re video-editing concepts (how this clip cuts into the next one, what supplementary footage to cut in) that a still keyframe image can''t represent, so they belong to the actual clip, not the Scene Blueprint.","content":{"fieldsSchema":[{"key":"direction","label":"Animation direction","type":"textarea","presets":["Slow push-in on the subject, hold at the end.","Static shot with a gentle handheld sway.","Pan left to right, following the product into frame.","Slow pull back to reveal the full scene.","Quick zoom in for emphasis, then hold steady."]},{"key":"duration","label":"Duration (sec)","type":"pill","options":["4","6","8","10","15","30"],"optionHelp":{"4":"What: a very short beat. When to use: for a quick cutaway or punchy insert. Example: a fast reaction shot between longer scenes.","6":"What: a brief, single-idea shot. When to use: for a simple action with no dialogue. Example: a quick unboxing moment.","8":"What: a short scene with time for one clear action or line. When to use: the standard length for a simple dialogue or action beat. Example: someone delivering one short line to camera.","10":"What: enough time for a small action plus a beat of reaction. When to use: the default for most standard scenes. Example: opening a product and reacting to it.","15":"What: enough time for a short sequence of actions or a fuller line of dialogue. When to use: for scenes with more than one beat. Example: picking up a product, examining it, then smiling.","30":"What: a longer, fuller scene. When to use: for a hero/opening scene or one with multiple beats and dialogue. Example: a full testimonial statement with setup and payoff."}},{"key":"broll","label":"B-roll","type":"pill","options":["None","Product Macro Shot","Product Spin","Slow-Motion Detail","Texture Close-up","Lifestyle Cutaway"],"optionHelp":{"None":"What: no supplementary footage. When to use: the main shot alone tells the story. Example: a simple testimonial clip, no product insert needed.","Product Macro Shot":"What: an extreme close-up insert of the product. When to use: to highlight fine detail like label or texture. Example: a close-up of a jar''s lid twisting open.","Product Spin":"What: a rotating, 360°-style shot of the product. When to use: to show its full form and branding. Example: a bottle slowly rotating to reveal its label.","Slow-Motion Detail":"What: a slowed-down insert emphasizing a small action. When to use: for a satisfying, premium-feel moment. Example: cream dripping slowly off a spoon.","Texture Close-up":"What: a tight shot on surface texture. When to use: to emphasize quality or ingredients. Example: a close-up on the weave of a fabric.","Lifestyle Cutaway":"What: a brief shot of the product in real use. When to use: to show it fitting into daily life. Example: someone casually sipping the drink at their desk."}},{"key":"transition","label":"Transition","type":"pill","options":["Hard Cut","Whip Pan","Match Cut","Fade","Zoom Transition"],"optionHelp":{"Hard Cut":"What: an instant cut with no effect. When to use: the default choice for most edits, keeps pacing snappy. Example: cutting straight from the product shot to the customer''s reaction.","Whip Pan":"What: a fast, blurred camera swipe between shots. When to use: for energetic, fast-paced transitions. Example: whipping from the kitchen to the dining table.","Match Cut":"What: a cut where the next shot''s composition or action matches the previous one. When to use: for a smooth, clever visual continuity. Example: a jar lid closing cuts to a door closing in the next scene.","Fade":"What: the shot gradually fades to/from black or the next shot. When to use: for a soft, emotional, or scene-ending transition. Example: fading out after the final call-to-action line.","Zoom Transition":"What: a quick zoom into or out of frame to transition. When to use: for punchy, modern social-style edits. Example: zooming into the logo before cutting to the next scene."}}]},"outputInstructions":[{"key":"master_final_production_render_prompt","label":"Master Final Production Render Prompt (per scene)","default":true,"text":"Create this scene''s final commercial-quality clip exactly from its approved Scene Blueprint.\n\nOBJECTIVE\n\nExecute this scene''s approved Scene Blueprint without inventing new story elements.\nMaintain complete continuity with the Character Sheets, Prop Sheets, Background Sheet, and Music Brief referenced for this scene.\n\nFOLLOW EXACTLY\n\n• This scene''s Blueprint\n• The Character Sheets featured in this scene\n• The Prop Sheets featured in this scene\n• This scene''s Background Sheet\n• The Music Brief\n\nCONTINUITY\n\n• Character identity\n• Hair & wardrobe\n• Props\n• Backgrounds\n• Time of day\n• Weather\n• Lighting\n• Camera direction\n• Screen direction\n• Dialogue timing\n• Emotion progression\n• Branding\n\nCINEMATOGRAPHY\n\n• Hollywood commercial quality\n• Premium advertising cinematography\n• Natural human motion\n• Realistic facial animation\n• Accurate lip sync (if dialogue exists)\n• Correct eye-line\n• Natural hand interactions\n• Realistic physics\n• Smooth camera movement\n• Consistent color grading\n• Feature-film lighting\n• Premium depth of field\n\nEDITING\n\n• Respect the planned shot duration\n• Honor the specified transition into/out of this clip\n• Include the specified B-roll insert, if any\n• Preserve pacing\n• Match music cues\n• Match sound effects\n• Broadcast-ready finish\n\nDO NOT\n\n• Change story\n• Add characters\n• Change wardrobe\n• Change props\n• Redesign locations\n• Change branding\n\nDIMENSIONS\n\nRender at the highest resolution Flow supports (minimum 1080p), in the aspect ratio matching the target platform — vertical 9:16 for Reels/Shorts/TikTok, 16:9 for YouTube/landscape.\n\nOUTPUT\n\nGenerate ONE polished, commercial-quality video clip for this scene, faithful to its approved Scene Blueprint and the Production Bible."}]},"final_video":{"label":"Final Video","note":"One per project, auto-created when Stage 4 locks (alongside the per-scene Movie pairing), never manually deletable. Upload-only — designer stitches every scene''s approved clip together externally and uploads the one finished result here. This is the only thing the customer''s public download for Stage 5 actually serves (see pay.ts) — the per-scene clips stay a production/designer artifact, not something the customer needs to see."}}}' WHERE stage = 5;
